  socialImage="/images/electric-blankets-vs-heated-throws.webp"
  socialImageAlt="An electric blanket controller beside a made bed with a heated throw folded over a nearby chair"
>
  <p>Electric blankets and heated throws both provide close, direct warmth, but they are designed for different situations.</p>
  <p>An electric blanket warms a bed. A heated throw warms an awake person sitting or reclining on a sofa, chair or at a desk. That difference determines the correct position, fit, controls, cable path and care routine.</p>
  <p>Choose by intended use rather than fabric, appearance or the number of heat settings. Products must only be used in the configuration specified by their manufacturer.</p>

  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>An electric blanket can pre-warm a cold bed without heating the whole <a href="/categories/bedroom/">bedroom</a>. A heated throw can warm one awake person without raising the temperature of an entire <a href="/categories/living-spaces/">living room</a>.</p>
  <p>Neither product is interchangeable. An underblanket is not used as an overblanket, and a heated throw is not used as an underblanket or pet mat.</p>
  <p>Fit, controller access, cord placement, cleaning instructions and the user’s ability to recognise excessive heat matter more than decorative finish.</p>

  <h2>Electric blankets warm a bed</h2>
  <p>In Australia, “electric blanket” commonly refers to a fitted or tie-down underblanket placed on the mattress beneath the sheet. Internal electrical elements warm the sleeping surface, and a controller selects the heat level.</p>
  <p>Some products are specifically designed to sit over a sleeper. Use each product only in its stated position. Do not put an underblanket over a person or fit an overblanket beneath the sheet.</p>
  <p>Install an underblanket flat and smooth so its heated area sits where the instructions show. Check whether a mattress protector goes above or below it, and keep the connector clear of the sleeper and bed frame. Deep bedding layers should not conceal a poor fit or force the blanket into creases.</p>
  <p>Current Australian product-safety advice recommends pre-warming the bed and switching the electric blanket off before sleeping. A timer or automatic shut-off is a backup control, not a reason to ignore that advice or the product instructions.</p>

  <h2>Heated throws warm an awake person</h2>
  <p>A heated throw is intended to drape loosely over an awake person who is sitting or reclining. It can suit a sofa, reading chair or desk where individual warmth is useful.</p>
  <p>A throw is usually smaller than bed bedding and has one controller. It should not be tucked tightly around a mattress, placed beneath a person or used while heavily folded or bunched.</p>
  <p>Allow the throw to rest naturally rather than wrapping it tightly around the body or pinning it beneath furniture. The user should be able to move it, reach the controller and notice if part of the fabric becomes unusually warm. Check that the lead does not pull when the person changes position.</p>
  <p>Do not use a heated throw as an underblanket or pet heating mat. A product designed and approved specifically for another configuration may have different instructions, but those instructions remain the deciding source.</p>

